<PageTitle>Format | BSAL</PageTitle>

<h1>Format</h1>

<section>
    <h2>Regular Season</h2>
    <p>
        The regular season consists of weekly best-of-one matchups played across a single division containing all league teams.
        Each week, teams are paired against one opponent — matchups are single elimination, meaning a loss ends your run for that week.
        Because all teams compete in one division with no conferences or subdivisions, not every team will face every other team over
        the course of a season. The number of regular season weeks is variable, ranging from 3 to 6, and is determined before each
        season based on the number of registered teams with the goal of producing the most competitive and enjoyable experience possible.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Format:</strong> Single elimination — a loss ends your run for that matchup.</li>
        <li><strong>Schedule:</strong> One matchup per team per week.</li>
        <li><strong>Division:</strong> All teams compete in a single division; there are no conferences or subdivisions.</li>
        <li><strong>Opponents:</strong> The schedule does not guarantee that every team will play every other team in a given season.</li>
        <li><strong>Season Length:</strong> The number of regular season weeks is variable, ranging from 3 to 6 weeks. The length is determined before each season based on the number of registered teams, with the goal of producing the most competitive and enjoyable experience possible.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Playoffs</h2>
    <p>
        At the conclusion of the regular season, as many teams as possible qualify for a single elimination playoff bracket. The bracket
        size is set to the largest power of two that fits within the total number of regular season teams — this ensures a clean bracket
        with no bye weeks. For example, if 12 teams compete in the regular season, the bracket holds 8 teams. Matchups are best-of-one,
        and teams are seeded based on their regular season win-loss record, with higher seeds earning more favorable bracket positions.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Format:</strong> Single elimination bracket — one loss and you're out.</li>
        <li><strong>Series Length:</strong> Best of one; each matchup is decided in a single game.</li>
        <li><strong>Qualification:</strong> The largest possible power-of-two field qualifies (e.g. 4, 8, 16) — as many teams as the bracket can hold without byes.</li>
        <li><strong>Seeding:</strong> Teams are seeded according to their regular season win-loss record.</li>
        <li><strong>Bracket Matchup Creation:</strong> Matchups are arranged using the 1-vs-N method — the top seed faces the lowest remaining seed, the second seed faces the second-lowest, and so on. This ensures the strongest teams are rewarded with the most favorable first-round matchups.</li>
    </ul>
</section>
